The numbers $146, a, b, c, d, 339$ form an arithmetic sequence (these numbers need not all be integers).<br>What is $a+b+c+d$ ?<br>Note. Numbers $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, \ldots, x_{n}$ form an arithmetic sequence, if the difference between consecutive terms is some constant $\Delta$, that is,<br>$$
\Delta=x_{2}-x_{1}=x_{3}-x_{2}=\cdots=x_{n}-x_{n-1}
$$<br>For such a sequence, $\Delta$ is called the common difference.<br>For example, $1,3,5,7$ form an arithmetic sequence with common difference 2.
